---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A calorie deficit is the gap between what your body burns in a day and what you eat. This
      weight loss calculator estimates your maintenance calories from a Mifflin-St Jeor basal
      metabolic rate scaled by activity, then subtracts the gap your chosen rate of loss needs.
      Because roughly 7,700 calories are stored in a kilogram of body fat, losing half a kilogram a
      week takes a deficit of about 550 calories a day. The daily calorie target is floored at your
      BMR, so an aggressive rate never produces a number you should not eat. Enter a goal weight and
      the timeline shows how many weeks the plan takes.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Calorie Deficit Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How a Calorie Deficit Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Energy balance is the whole mechanism. Eat less than you burn and your body makes up the
      difference from stored energy, mostly fat; eat more and it stores the surplus. The size of the
      gap sets the rate. The calculator works backwards from the rate you want: it converts your
      weekly target into a daily energy shortfall, subtracts that from maintenance calories, and
      gives you the number to eat. Everything upstream of that is an estimate, which is why the tool
      shows maintenance, the deficit, and the resting floor side by side rather than a single figure
      to obey.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="choosing-a-rate">
    <h2>Choosing a Rate of Loss <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A sensible range is 0.5 to 1 percent of body weight a week. At 80 kg that is 0.4 to 0.8 kg,
      needing a deficit of roughly 440 to 880 calories a day. Slower is not weaker: gentle deficits
      preserve muscle better, leave enough energy to train hard, and survive a bad week without
      collapsing. Aggressive rates lose more lean tissue, and the practical failure mode is not
      danger but abandonment, usually inside a fortnight. Pick the rate you could still be running in
      two months.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="adaptation">
    <h2>Why the Target Moves <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A lighter body is cheaper to run. Lose 5 kg and your maintenance falls by roughly 50 to 100
      calories a day, which quietly narrows the deficit you set at the start. Metabolic adaptation
      shrinks it a little further, as the body trims non-exercise movement and resting expenditure in
      response to sustained energy shortage. Neither effect is dramatic, and neither is a broken
      metabolism, but together they explain most stalls at week eight or ten.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Recalculate as you go">
      <p>Re-run the numbers every 4 to 5 kg. The deficit that worked at your starting weight is a smaller deficit now, and eventually it is maintenance. Weekly averages, not single weigh-ins, are what should trigger the change.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p><strong>Man, 30, 90 kg, 180 cm, moderately active, 0.5 kg a week.</strong> For example, maintenance lands near 2,900 calories, so the target is about 2,350 with a deficit of 550.</p>
    <p><strong>The same man asking for 1 kg a week.</strong> For example, the deficit would be 1,100, putting intake near 1,800, which is above his BMR of about 1,800 but leaves almost no margin.</p>
    <p><strong>Woman, 35, 65 kg, 165 cm, lightly active, 1 kg a week.</strong> For example, maintenance is near 1,900, so a 1,100 deficit would fall below her BMR of about 1,350; the target holds at the floor and the achievable rate drops to roughly 0.5 kg a week.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Choosing the most aggressive rate available">
      <p>
        The fastest option looks like the efficient one, and it is the option most often abandoned.
        A deficit you hold for four months beats one you hold for nine days, and the slower plan
        usually arrives at the goal weight first because it never restarts.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Reading one weigh-in as progress">
      <p>
        Daily weight moves with water, salt, glycogen, and food still in transit, easily by a
        kilogram in either direction. A fortnight of genuine fat loss can hide behind that noise.
        Weigh at the same time each day and judge the weekly average.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="The timeline is a best case">
      <p>Weeks to goal assumes the deficit holds every day and your burn stays put. Neither is true, so a 20 week estimate often runs 24. Use it to tell a two month project from a six month one. Everything here runs in your browser and never leaves your device.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
